# TSan suppressions for `make test-tsan`. One entry per deliberate protocol, each with the reason it is
# correct, the way a corpus allowlist carries its §8 citation (docs/notes/gates.md, "TSan"). A blanket entry
# and an entry for something nobody understood are both worse than a red gate: an entry matches any frame of
# either stack, by substring, so it hides every future race under that function too.

# The protocol inline cache of a dispatch site is a seqlock (eval.c, proto_ic_find/proto_ic_fill): the reader
# copies the entry without a lock and keeps it only if ic->seq is unchanged and even across the copy, so a torn
# read is discarded rather than used. TSan reports the payload of every seqlock; the odd/even counter is what
# makes it correct, and it is a release/acquire atomic. A fill racing another fill gives up on the CAS.
race:proto_ic_find
race:proto_ic_fill

# A `def` while another thread analyzes or runs a form that reads the var's flags. Every `def` stores `macro`
# and `dynamic` (eval.c, the def node), a redefinition the same values; the analyzer's macro test and a var
# node's dynamic test read them without a lock. Redefinition is a dev-time operation that is not ordered against
# a running call (NOTES "Analyzer and evaluator", "Concurrent `def` against `deref` is unsafe"); for the flags
# themselves either value is a valid answer, and each is its own byte, so neither write can lose the other's.
race:clj_var_is_macro
race:clj_var_set_macro
race:clj_var_is_dynamic
race:clj_var_set_dynamic

# The cycle collector reads the slots of a shared volatile, object array or lazy seq without a lock while their
# writers store without one (cc.c, visit_lockfree; design §7, the lock-free protocol). A read that races a store
# is never used: the writer's barrier and watch check after the store make the collector's re-check of the
# owner's WATCH fail, and frees are deferred while a collection runs, so even a stale pointer meets live memory.
race:visit_lockfree
